<description>
Reorder the vertical color-major blocks while preserving every block's internal pixels.

Input structure
The grid is divided into vertical blocks, each with a unique dominant color.
Small marker pixels from other block colors may appear inside the blocks.

Rule
If adjacent shared-color marker rows read exactly as the current block order
from right to left, reverse the blocks. Otherwise move the leftmost block to
the far right and shift the remaining blocks left.

Key insight
The transformation acts on whole vertical blocks, so marker pixels move with
their source block instead of being recolored or independently rearranged.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_in_order",
  "src": "vertical color-major blocks s, including their internal marker pixels",
  "dst": "left-to-right output block slots d with the same widths as the ordered source blocks",
  "match_cond": "rank(s) == rank(d) after applying the block-order rule",
  "transform": "copy each whole source block unchanged into its assigned output slot",
  "multi_step": "default order is a one-block left shift; if adjacent shared-color marker rows read the current block colors right-to-left, use reverse order"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 7-9 rows x 10-15 cols
  blocks:
    count: 3-4 vertical color-major blocks spanning full height, each with a unique dominant color
    widths: 3-7 cols each; all block colors distinct; widths need not be equal
  markers:
    count_per_block: exactly N-1 (one per other block color), each a single pixel
    row_rule: within a block, each marker is on a distinct row
    col_position: each marker occupies a single interior column of its block [observed]
  marker_alignment:
    aligned_case: all markers of the same foreign color share the same row across all blocks (4/6 examples)
    unaligned_case: markers of a given foreign color may be on different rows in different blocks (2/6 examples)
  permutation_rule:
    reverse: if aligned AND N marker-rows are consecutive AND marker colors top-to-bottom equal block colors right-to-left -> reverse block order
    left_shift: otherwise -> move leftmost block to far right, shift rest left
  output: same grid dimensions; blocks reordered per permutation rule, each block's internal pixels preserved exactly

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct construction (input is the complete artifact)
  steps:
    1. Pick H in [7,9], N in [3,4], and N distinct colors
    2. Pick N block widths in [3,7]; W = sum of widths
    3. Fill each block column-span with its dominant color
    4. Decide permutation type (left_shift or reverse)
    5. Choose N distinct marker rows (one per foreign color)
    6. For reverse - make marker rows consecutive and order marker colors to match reversed block order
    7. For left_shift - either scatter marker rows non-consecutively, or use non-reverse color ordering
    8. Place one marker pixel of each foreign color in each block (on that color's designated row), at an interior column
    9. For the unaligned variant (left_shift only), optionally shift some foreign-color rows independently per block
    10. Verify solver produces correct output
